Port the term region and the worker tables to wasm32

Gruszka reserves its term region (16 + 8 + 8 bytes for every u32 term id:
128 GiB of address space) and its worker tables (values, environments,
slots, spines: 2^28 to 2^32 entries each) with mmap(MAP_NORESERVE), so
that pages are made on first write and a term read needs no bounds check.
wasm32-wasip1 has no mmap and a 4 GiB address space (ID_SPACE * 16 does
not even fit a 32-bit usize), so there (and in --cfg fuzzing builds, so
native replays agree) the region is a zeroed heap block that is never
freed and a worker table a heap block freed with the table (pushed before
it is read, so not zeroed; `vm::release` is only called on unix already),
the region holds
2^19 term ids (16 MiB), the layout's block and overflow arenas are at
least 2^16 ids instead of 2^20, and the worker tables hold 2^16 values,
2^16 environments, 2^18 slots and 2^18 spine words. ID_LIMIT (2^31) stays
the bound that tags LL_POOL references. A file whose MAIN leaves no room
for the arenas declines ("no room for block arenas"), and a declaration
that fills a table declines, as at the native limits. Reads stay at live
ids (MAIN or the current arena: `Worker::term_live`, asserted in
release-da builds; the arena fixtures and the smoke corpus pass a
release-da build of the harness), so no read reaches past the block.
`layout` also starts handing slabs out from the first again, so that one
process can lay out (and check) one export after another, as wasmfuzz
runs input after input in one instance.
Upstream: none (gruszka has no wasm target; no issues or PRs, checked 2026-11-22).

